{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it shall be if you heed, etc.</b> The meaning of <b>וְהָיָה</b> is the language of joy, and it is a conditional matter: if there will be joy of mitzvah. That is what it says — <b>if you heed, you shall heed</b>, etc. — as they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 30:), that Solomon praised nothing but the joy of mitzvah; but regarding other joys he said (Ecclesiastes 2:2) ״וּלְשִׂמְחָה מַה זֹּה עוֹשָׂה״. And he doubled to say <b>hear, you shall hear</b> in the manner of their saying, of blessed memory (Berakhot 55:), on the verse (Daniel 2:21) ״יָהֵב חָכְמְתָא לְחַכִּימִין״ — that the Holy One, blessed be He, gives wisdom only to one who already has wisdom. That is what it says: <b>״אִם שָׁמוֹעַ״</b> — meaning, that you should have a listening heart — <b>״תִּשְׁמְעוּ״</b> [you shall hear]." "<b>To love, etc., and to serve Him, etc., and I will give, etc.</b> Meaning: according to what has preceded us, that the reward of mitzvot is not paid out in this world — Scripture informs us that if they love HaShem, etc., with all their heart, etc., they will merit even in this world as well. That is what is intended in the statement ״וְנָתַתִּי״ with the addition of the vav, to awaken you that this is only an addition upon the essence, which is the reward of the eternal and everlasting world. Also, He alluded that even though sustenance is included among the things that do not depend on merit (Mo'ed Katan 28:), nevertheless when they serve HaShem as required, the constellation does not rule over them, and they will eat and be satisfied even with physical good." "<b>With all your heart and with all your soul.</b> And it does not say ״with all your might״ in the order it said in the section of Shema (Deuteronomy 6:5), because the section of Shema was stated in the singular, and it is possible that there may be found an individual whose wealth is dearer to him than his soul. But the totality of the community, with whom He is speaking — it is inconceivable that their wealth would be dearer to them than their souls, for all that a man has he will give for his life. Therefore, when He said ״with all your soul,״ all the more so ״with all your might.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:39.537109881Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}